533:     "control + census words re-read by the tree's own disassembler (not the 3 cand words)")
534: 
535: # ================================ WHY THE SIBLING ROWS ARE NOT HERE =============
536: #
537: #  The witness this file is derived from is a FAMILY instrument: it also rows
538: #  X(rldimi) and X(rlwimi), which carry their OWN measured defects (ppcrldimimask,
539: #  ppcrlwimiwidth) that #522 does not touch and must not touch.  Shipping those rows
540: #  in a DETECTOR would make it PERMANENTLY RED until two unrelated rounds land --
541: #  a detector that cannot be green is a different fault from the one it was written
542: #  to catch, and a gate row over it would have to be ignored, which is how a gate
543: #  stops being read at all.
544: #
545: #  So this detector is scoped to X(rldicl).  The three sibling rows stay in the
546: #  witness at regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/, which is the EVIDENCE for those two
547: #  filed rows and is correctly red there.  When either is fixed, its rows move here
548: #  or into a detector of its own -- that is a decision for those rounds, not this one.
549: #
550: #  MEASURED: witness 19/27 committed and 24/27 fixed, the five flipped rows being
551: #  exactly the ones kept below.  **THIS FILE HAS 19 ROWS, NOT 24** [19-row era: 24 since
552: #  pass 2, see #565's note below] -- an earlier draft of this note carried the witness's
553: #  own post-fix count straight across, which a pass-2 seat caught.  It is 19/19 on the
554: #  fix and 14/19 pre-fix (killtable_20260910/SCORES.txt).
555: #  [#565, 2026-10-18: TRUE OF THE 19-ROW FILE IT DESCRIBED, AND FALSE SINCE PASS 2 -- the
556: #  five rows under `ROWS ADDED BY PASS 2' below make it 24, which gate_ppc.sh now pins:
557: #  24/24 on the fix and 16/24 on the pre-fix HEAD (killtable_20260910/t2/SCORES.txt),
558: #  16/24 again on a pre-#522 build at #565 (gatewire_20261018/controls/
559: #  rldicl_prefix_02af7f3e.log).  The 19-row figures are in killtable_20260910/t1/SCORES.txt;
560: #  the bare `killtable_20260910/SCORES.txt' above names no file.  This block also stood
561: #  here TWICE, verbatim, from e777ff06 until #565 deleted the copy after the pass-2 rows.]
562: 
563: # ================== ROWS ADDED BY PASS 2, WHICH FOUND TWO TOTAL ESCAPES =========
564: #
565: #  The 19-row instrument scored 19/19 against BOTH of these mutants -- total escapes,
566: #  MEASURED by the pass-2 measuring seat, not predicted:
567: #
568: #    M_SH5   drop the `sh5' bit from the SH decode.  Every rldicl with SH >= 32 then
569: #            rotates by SH-32.  *** THE INSTRUMENT WAS BLIND BY CONSTRUCTION: the
570: #            census uses an ALL-ONES source, and ROTL64(all-ones, n) is all-ones for
571: #            every n, so all 126 SH-varying cells compute the same value whatever the
572: #            rotate amount.  The only rotate-sensitive rows were zx32 (SH=0) and rot8
573: #            (SH=8) -- BOTH BELOW 32, so the sh5 bit was never load-bearing anywhere. ***
574: #            And it matters: the manual's simplified mnemonics make `srdi rA,rS,n' EQUAL
575: #            to `rldicl rA,rS,64-n,n', so EVERY logical right shift by 1..32 has SH >= 32.
576: #            Measured: under M_SH5 `clrldi' stays CORRECT while `srdi r5,r3,1' is wrong.
577: #            The one form the old instrument pinned is the one the mutant gets right.
578: #
579: #    M_CR0   feed update_cr0 the UNMASKED rotated value.  *** THIS IS NOT AN INVENTED
580: #            MUTANT: it is the token X(rldicr) ships, CORRECTLY, ten lines below -- there
581: #            `tmp' IS the destination.  A future editor harmonising the two handlers
582: #            writes exactly this. ***  The old instrument could not see it because it
583: #            emitted rc=0 on every encoding, leaving the whole record-form path unrun.
584: #
585: #  CR0 is a THREE-valued channel here and each candidate lands in a different value:
586: #    the fix -> GT (0x40000000) | the defect -> EQ (0x20000000) | M_CR0 -> LT (0x80000000)
587: #  so one row separates all three states.  These rows also redden under M_ORIG, so they
588: #  satisfy floor (a)/(b) as well as killing the (d)-shaped mutant.
589: #
590: #  In their OWN session, so the control session's disasm cross-check lists are untouched.
591: xregs, _, xT, xhow = session(
592:     seed([(SRC, RS_VAL)] + [(r, SENT) for r in DESTS]),
593:     [RLDICL(8, SRC, 63, 0),            # rotldi r8,r3,63 -- SH=63 NEEDS the sh5 bit
594:      RLDICL(11, SRC, 32, 32),          # high-word extract -- SH=32 NEEDS the sh5 bit
595:      enc_md(0, SRC, 10, 0, 32, rc=1)]) # rldicl. r10,r3,0,32 -- THE RECORD FORM
596: _cr = re.findall(r"cr[ \t]+= (0x[0-9a-fA-F]+)", xT)
597: _crv = int(_cr[-1], 16) if _cr else -1
598: row("cand session", xhow, "exit0", "the candidate-row session ran to `quit'")
599: row("cr parsed", "yes" if _cr else "no", "yes",
600:     "NEGATIVE CONTROL on the apparatus: the cr regex must MATCH something")
601: row("sh63 rldicl", hex(xregs.get(8, -1)), hex(want_rldicl(RS_VAL, 63, 0)),
602:     "SH=63: pins the sh5 bit of the SH decode")
603: row("sh32 rldicl", hex(xregs.get(11, -1)), hex(want_rldicl(RS_VAL, 32, 32)),
604:     "SH=32,MB=32 high-word extract: pins the sh5 bit again, on a masked cell")
605: row("rc cr0", hex(_crv), hex(0x40000000),
606:     "rldicl. -> CR0=GT.  defect gives EQ(0x2...), update_cr0(cpu,tmp) gives LT(0x8...)")
607: 
608: #  =============================== CONTRACT (clause (e)) =========================
609: #
610: #  WHAT THIS DETECTOR IS DESIGNED TO CATCH, and what it is NOT.  The boundary below
611: #  is MEASURED -- every entry was established by a mutant that was built and scored,
612: #  not by reasoning about the row set.
613: #
614: #  CAUGHT:  the mask variable; the mask bit positions; the mask end bound; `&' -> `|';
615: #           the mask ignored entirely; the `mb'-field decode INCLUDING its high bit;
616: #           rotate-versus-shift (the census sees SHL(all-ones,n) != all-ones); the
617: #           rotate AMOUNT at SH=0, 8, 32 and 63; the destination width; the `ra'-field
618: #           decode for ra >= 16; and the record form's CR0 result.
619: #
620: #  NOT CAUGHT, disclosed rather than left to be discovered:
621: #    * `rs'-field edits that are the identity on r3.  EVERY rldicl this file executes
622: #      reads rS = r3, so `(iw >> 21) & 31' -> `& 15' escapes.  Low plausibility, named
623: #      because it is a real property of the design rather than an oversight.
624: #    * edits conditioned on `ra == 0'.
625: #    * rotate-amount edits that are the identity at ALL FOUR rowed SH values.
626: #
627: #  *** AND THE 190-CELL CENSUS IS NARROWER THAN ITS HEADLINE NUMBER SUGGESTS. ***  It
628: #  drives an ALL-ONES source deliberately, so that every mask window is non-empty and
629: #  "190 of 190" means something.  The same choice makes ROTL64 the IDENTITY, so the
630: #  census is a function of MB ALONE: the 63 cells of its SH sweep are 63 copies of the
631: #  same mask case.  **The census pins MASK(MB,63).  It does not pin the rotate.**  The
632: #  rotate is pinned by the value rows -- zx32, rot8, sh32 and sh63 -- and a genuine
633: #  two-axis census would need a second source value.  This was found by a pass-2 seat
634: #  after the round's own PRETABLE claimed the census "walks the cross-product"; it does
635: #  not, and the corrected sentence is the one that belongs in a contract.
636: 
637: # ================================================================ SCORE + DONE
638: npass = sum(1 for _, ok in rows if ok)
639: print("")
640: print("SCORE %d/%d  elapsed=%.1fs  binary=%s  label=%s"
641:       % (npass, len(rows), time.time() - t0, BIN, LABEL))
642: print("FAILING: " + ", ".join(n for n, ok in rows if not ok))
643: print("PPC_RLDICL_PROBE_DONE")

 succeeded in 200ms:
130: #  in the harness's own control flow.
131: #
132: #  So a gate_skip AFTER failures is a FAIL, not a skip: the skip cannot un-record
133: #  what already failed. A gate_skip with a clean slate stays a genuine skip (a
134: #  preflight that could not run at all -- need_file/need_exec). degrade() was
135: #  already correct: gate_end() tests _fails before _degraded, so a degraded gate
136: #  with recorded failures already reports FAIL. Nothing there to change.
137: gate_skip() {
138:     echo
139:     if [ "${_fails:-0}" != 0 ]; then
140:         echo "${C_BAD}$GATE_NAME: FAIL${C_OFF} ($_fails of $_checks checks failed" \
141:              "before this section could not run: $*)"
142:         exit 1
143:     fi
144:     echo "${C_SKIP}$GATE_NAME: SKIP${C_OFF} -- $*"; exit $EXIT_SKIP
145: }
146: 
147: need_file() {
148:     for f in "$@"; do
149:         [ -e "$f" ] || gate_skip "missing required input: $f"
150:     done
151: }
152: 
153: need_exec() {
154:     for f in "$@"; do
155:         [ -x "$f" ] || gate_skip "missing or non-executable binary: $f"
156:     done
157: }
158: 
